## 🎯 Room Objective
Learn how to efficiently search the internet and leverage specialized cybersecurity search engines, threat intelligence platforms, and technical documentation.

---

## 🔍 Specialized Search Tools & Documentation

| Tool / Platform | Primary Purpose | Key Details |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Shodan** | IoT & Internet-Connected Device Search | Continuously scans the internet to index exposed devices, open ports, services, and approximate geographic locations. |
| **VirusTotal** | Malware & Indicator Analysis | Analyzes suspicious files, URLs, domains, and file hashes across multiple antivirus engines to determine if they are malicious and assess their severity. |
| **Vulnerability Databases (NVD / CVE)** | Tracking Known Vulnerabilities | Standardized catalog of publicly disclosed security flaws formatted as **`CVE-YEAR-NUMBER`** (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures). |
| **GitHub** | Exploit & Patch Research | Searching a `CVE-YEAR-NUMBER` on GitHub helps security researchers and red teamers locate Proof-of-Concept (PoC) exploit scripts, affected source code, or vendor patches. |
| **`man` (Manual Pages)** | Built-in CLI Technical Documentation | Running `man <command>` directly in the Linux terminal displays the official manual, usage syntax, and available flags for command-line tools. |

---

## 💡 Key Takeaways
Effective cybersecurity work relies heavily on knowing *where* to look—both online and locally. Tools like **Shodan** expose internet-facing attack surfaces, **VirusTotal** speeds up triage on suspicious indicators, and pairing **CVE databases** with **GitHub** bridges the gap between discovering a vulnerable service and understanding how it works. Meanwhile, built-in **`man` pages** provide fast, offline technical documentation right inside the terminal when learning new CLI tools.
